%U A335978 909,990,999,1089,1179,1188,1269,1278,1359,1368,1449,1458,1539,1548,1629,1638,1719,1728,1800,1809,1818,1890,1908,1980,1989,1998,2079
%N A335978 Numbers m of the form abs(k - reverse(k)) for at least one k.
%C A335978 All terms are divisible by 9.
%C A335978 Let f(k) = k - reverse(k). Then f(reverse(k)) = -f(k), since f(reverse(k)) = reverse(k) - reverse(reverse(k)) = reverse(k) - k = - (k - reverse(k)) = -f(k).
%C A335978 Iteration of the function f(k) = k - reverse(k) leads to A072140, A072141, A072142, and A072143.
%C A335978 From _Charles L. Hohn_, Aug 04 2026: (Start)
%C A335978 Consider any number abcdefg where each letter [a-g] is some digit [0..9], letting h = abs(a-g), j = abs(b-f), and k = abs(c-e) (each also always a digit [0..9]):
%C A335978   abs(a00000g - g00000a) = h00000[-h] = hhhhhh * 9,
%C A335978   abs(0b000f0 - 0f000b0) = 0j000[-j]0 = 0jjjj0 * 9,
%C A335978   abs(00c0e00 - 00e0c00) = 00k0[-k]00 = 00kk00 * 9, and
%C A335978   abs(000d000 - 000d000) = 0.
%C A335978   Therefore, abcdefg - gfedcba always equals (+- hhhhhh +- 0jjjj0 +- 00kk00) * 9.
%C A335978   Extending this model to any number of digits, this sequence comprises exactly all nonnegative numbers that can be expressed as such. (End)
%H A335978 Charles L. Hohn, <a href="/A335978/b335978.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A335978 Michael P. Greaney, <a href="https://plus.maths.org/content/arithmetic-made-easy-reversible-numbers">Remarkable Reversible Numbers</a>, +plus magazine, (September 29, 2015).
%o A335978 (PARI) is_a335978(k) = is_a334145(k/9) \\ from A334145 - _Charles L. Hohn_, Jul 31 2026
%Y A335978 Cf. A056965, A067030, A072140, A072141, A072142, A072143.
%Y A335978 Dividing by 9 gives A334145.
